course: dictionary
lesson: 8803
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. The word shaver is a good example. It can be a tool, an old-fashioned name for a boy, or a person who shaves. Look at the words around it to find the right meaning.
explain: Read this sentence: «Water, especially with salt or soap in it, lets electricity pass through it and through you, which is why UK bathrooms usually have no ordinary sockets, only shaver sockets.» Here shaver means the electric tool, because it is linked to sockets and to a bathroom. The clue is the word socket.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. Swap in «a young boy» and it makes no sense about bathrooms. Swap in «an electric tool» and it does. Always check the clues first, then test the meaning.
fact: A word can have several meanings, so always check the context.
fact: A shaver is an electric tool with small moving blades that cuts hair close to the skin.
fact: «A young shaver» is an old-fashioned, informal way to say a boy or young lad.
fact: A «shaver socket» is a special bathroom plug in the UK, used so that water and electricity stay safely apart.
fact: Safety rule: children should never play with shavers or razors, because the blades are sharp.
exercise: What is an electric shaver? | A small boat for fishing | A sharp knife for cutting bread | *An electric tool for cutting hair close to the skin | A kind of kettle
exercise: Grandad calls Tom «a young shaver». What does he mean? | A bald man | *A young boy or lad | An electric tool for the bathroom | A sharp blade for cutting wood
exercise: Find and copy one word from «lets electricity pass through it» that means go through. | electricity | water | bathrooms | *pass
test: Why do UK bathrooms usually have shaver sockets instead of ordinary sockets? | *Because water can let electricity pass through it, so special sockets keep people safer | Because shavers need more power than a kettle | Because ordinary sockets are too big for shavers | Because shavers only work in the dark
test: What is a shaver socket? | A socket for boiling the kettle | *A special bathroom plug in the UK | A small blade used at school | A tool for cutting hair
exam: Why should children not play with shavers or razors? | Because they are only used for cooking | Because they are too heavy to lift | *Because the blades are sharp | Because they make a loud noise only at night
